sjdevnull@yahoo.com wrote:
> 
> Is giving up 3.9 points of damage worth the occasional probe, scare,
> stun, or cancellation? 

The probe surely not; that's why its probability is increased when
enchanting Magicbane. The other effects? It depends, IMO.

> I think not, especially if I have a
> stethoscope--I can 1-turn E with Magicbane if I need things scared,
> which is far more reliable. 

Yes the E-word is a strong argument, I think _the_ strong argument,
because the damage difference is not that significant. And from the
nasties that you may want to scare the minotauri (besides maybe the
elven king) are the worst, because they won't be affected by that
word _and_ they are very tough so that you need a lot hits; Magicbane
is not very good at that (ie. damaging), neither at +2 nor at +7. A
magical attack can be advantageous here. Or otherwise a temporary
switch to a better weapon or spell (before switching back to the
curse absorption), or simply by teleporting the nasty away. What
about cancelling a spellcaster? That's also fine if it succeeds as
a side effect. Or cancelling any other of the special attack nasties
(specific monsters from c, D, Y, w, g, V, A, h, P, R - there are a
lot, and nasties scare me); you will occasionally get it for free.

> Stunning's even less useful than scaring,
> and cancellation is one of those things that's either worthless or you
> _need_ it to work, so relying on magicbane for it just isn't sensible.

Unreliability is another good argument. Against, eg., the minotauri,
Magicbane requires far to much damaging hits. And against the other
nasties? (Nasties is what I have mainly in focus.) In practise I've
observed that there's no problem at all with 9.5 points of damage
(compared to 3.9 more points) for most of your targets. I know there
are other opinions but that's what my personal statistics tell me;
either I need a much heavier damage attack or it suffices for most
targets. The line where that small difference matters isn't well
populated. (Granted, that may depend on the personal playing style.)

> Magicbane's best effects (magic resistance, curse resistance, 1-turn
> E) work just as well with it at +7 as +0.

At the point where you rely on curse absorption, during the homerun,
I rarely needed the E-word. But Magicbane's various properties makes
it an universal weapon for the whole game, mainly early and late game.
Where you want to make much damage switch to other means. There's not
really a point in enchanting Magicbane for damage. (IMO; as always.)

(Hmm.., again made a lot words for a discussion that seems to me to
be quite academic, and the opinions on both sides yet fixed.)

